WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Validation asks whether the Bayesian network behaves well enough for its intended purpose.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.920
Compare predictions with observed outcomes when data is available.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.130
Check probability calibration: when the model says an event has about a 70 percent chance,

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.952
events in that range should happen roughly 70 percent

00:00:14.952 --> 00:00:17.800
of the time over many comparable cases.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.315
Calibration is not about one case.

00:00:19.315 --> 00:00:22.425
It is about probability quality across repeated use.

00:00:22.425 --> 00:00:26.945
Sensitivity analysis asks how much outputs change when assumptions or probability values

00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:27.670
change.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.660
If a small change in one CPT row dramatically changes a decision,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.120
that row deserves review.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.160
Test unusual and boundary scenarios.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.035
Review whether important variables are missing.

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:40.080
Check probability sources and document whether they came from data, expert judgment,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.869
literature, simulation, or a temporary assumption.

00:00:42.869 --> 00:00:44.375
Bayesian networks have limits.

00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:46.600
Poor data can produce poor estimates.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.320
Incorrect graph assumptions can distort inference.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.275
Hidden common causes can create misleading relationships.

00:00:51.275 --> 00:00:53.000
Rare events may be hard to estimate.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.915
Human probability estimates can be inconsistent.

00:00:54.915 --> 00:00:59.130
A model may perform differently for different populations, environments, product versions,

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:00.880
or operating conditions.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.495
Outputs can appear more certain than the evidence justifies.

00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:05.480
Responsible use requires maintenance.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:06.550
Track model versions.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.460
Monitor data and environment changes.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:11.300
Reassess the network when relationships change.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.510
Document purpose, owners, assumptions, limitations, review criteria,

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:16.635
missing-data handling, and escalation paths.

00:01:16.635 --> 00:01:20.661
For consequential decisions, use human oversight and appropriate domain review.

00:01:20.661 --> 00:01:24.595
A Bayesian network can support decision making, but it should not be treated as an oracle.

00:01:24.595 --> 00:01:29.030
It is a model whose usefulness depends on fit, evidence, validation, and governance.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:32.020
Calibration and sensitivity work should be understandable to stakeholders.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.700
A technical team may inspect error metrics and posterior distributions,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.890
while a manager may need to know whether the model is reliable enough for

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:39.520
a workflow.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.120
Both views matter.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.620
Responsible reporting should explain where the model performs well, where it is uncertain,

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.720
and which inputs most affect the result.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.240
That makes the model easier to govern and safer to improve.
